\DOC LOCAL_DOUBLE_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_DOUBLE_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-execution goal across an inlined
modular-doubling code block, replacing the current state variable by a
successor one and recording the doubling result as a new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects which register/offset convention (code
variant) of the doubling block is stepped over; the second, a {string list},
names the output and input quantities of the doubling (the result name and the
name of the value being doubled), and thereby fixes the names used in the
generated correctness assumption. The conclusion keeps its shape — an
{eventually} claim about a postcondition — but its final state argument becomes
a fresh later state variable. All state-dependent assumptions (program-counter
value, code-loaded fact, register contents, memory-contents equations, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state) are discharged and
re-added with the new state variable: the program-counter value is advanced,
the {MAYCHANGE} relation is extended by the components the block writes, and
extra register and memory-contents assumptions are added for the newly written
buffer. A new assumption of the form {t < u ==> ...}, stating that the computed
result equals twice the input under a bound hypothesis on the input, is also
introduced. Purely state-independent assumptions (nonoverlapping and arithmetic
facts) are left untouched.
{
   A ?- t
   =============================  LOCAL_DOUBLE_4_TAC n ["result"; "input"]
   A' ?- t'
}
\noindent where the state variable in {t} and in the state-dependent part of
{A} is advanced, giving {t'} and {A'}, and {A'} additionally contains the
doubling fact and the new buffer-contents assumption.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of forward step, on goals of
this one assembly-verification form.

\ENDDOC
